    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 5\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[24, 27, 12], [6, 25, 14], [6, 15, 15], [20, 7, 8], [9, 10, 30]]\n    machine_assignments = [[2, 0, 1], [1, 2, 0], [2, 1, 0], [1, 0, 2], [1, 0, 2]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Coordinated scheduling across specialized workstations to shorten end-to-end completion time\n\nOverview\nOur facility operates a classic job shop environment with multiple specialized machines (workstations) and a portfolio of customer orders (jobs) moving through the plant. Each job follows a defined route consisting of a series of operations performed in a fixed sequence across different machines. Every operation is tied to a specific machine and requires a known amount of processing time.\n\nThe scheduling challenge\nBecause several jobs compete for access to the same machines at overlapping times, we must determine the order in which each machine processes its queued operations and when each operation should start. Decisions made early in a job\u2019s route affect downstream availability, creating the risk of idle machines, growing queues, and uneven loading if the sequence is not chosen carefully. Processing times vary by operation, so a sequence that looks efficient on one machine can increase waiting time on another, extending the overall completion time for all jobs.\n\nOperating requirements and rules\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time; there is no parallel processing on a single machine.\n- Fixed job routing: Within any job, operations must be completed strictly in their defined order; a downstream operation cannot begin until its predecessor has finished.\n- Non-interruption (no preemption): Once an operation starts on a machine, it runs to completion without pause or transfer.\n- Machine- and time-specific tasks: Each operation is assigned to a particular machine with a known processing duration that must be respected.\n\nGoal\nCreate a feasible, shop-floor-ready schedule that assigns a start time and sequence for every operation on every machine, respects the rules above, and completes all jobs in the shortest possible total time (minimizes the overall makespan). Success will be measured by the projected finish time for all jobs and the degree to which we keep machines productively utilized without causing avoidable queues.\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm the operation routing (machine assignments) and processing durations for every job and operation.\n- Evaluate alternative sequences for each machine\u2019s queue to reduce waiting between operations within jobs.\n- Propose a detailed run plan with planned start/finish times by operation and machine, and report the resulting projected completion time and any expected bottlenecks.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 5\n- Number of machines (workstations): 3\n- Operation-to-machine assignments by job and sequence: Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 2\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 1]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 2]\n- Operation processing times by job and sequence: Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 3 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 24, Op2: 27, Op3: 12]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 6, Op2: 25, Op3: 14]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 6, Op2: 15, Op3: 15]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 20, Op2: 7, Op3: 8]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: 9, Op2: 10, Op3: 30]",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Coordinated scheduling across specialized workstations to shorten end-to-end completion time\n\nOverview\nOur facility operates a classic job shop environment with multiple specialized machines (workstations) and a portfolio of customer orders (jobs) moving through the plant. Each job follows a defined route consisting of a series of operations performed in a fixed sequence across different machines. Every operation is tied to a specific machine and requires a known amount of processing time.\n\nThe scheduling challenge\nBecause several jobs compete for access to the same machines at overlapping times, we must determine the order in which each machine processes its queued operations and when each operation should start. Decisions made early in a job\u2019s route affect downstream availability, creating the risk of idle machines, growing queues, and uneven loading if the sequence is not chosen carefully. Processing times vary by operation, so a sequence that looks efficient on one machine can increase waiting time on another, extending the overall completion time for all jobs.\n\nOperating requirements and rules\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can process only one operation at a time; there is no parallel processing on a single machine.\n- Fixed job routing: Within any job, operations must be completed strictly in their defined order; a downstream operation cannot begin until its predecessor has finished.\n- Non-interruption (no preemption): Once an operation starts on a machine, it runs to completion without pause or transfer.\n- Machine- and time-specific tasks: Each operation is assigned to a particular machine with a known processing duration that must be respected.\n\nGoal\nCreate a feasible, shop-floor-ready schedule that assigns a start time and sequence for every operation on every machine, respects the rules above, and completes all jobs in the shortest possible total time (minimizes the overall makespan). Success will be measured by the projected finish time for all jobs and the degree to which we keep machines productively utilized without causing avoidable queues.\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm the operation routing (machine assignments) and processing durations for every job and operation.\n- Evaluate alternative sequences for each machine\u2019s queue to reduce waiting between operations within jobs.\n- Propose a detailed run plan with planned start/finish times by operation and machine, and report the resulting projected completion time and any expected bottlenecks.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Operation-to-machine assignments by job and sequence: {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}\n- Operation processing times by job and sequence: {PROCESSING_TIMES}"
